This is template code that could be used for a yaw case study. It is almost entirely the same as the aspectRatio study, so I would recommend reading the README for that study before running this one.
The primary difference between the models is that this one includes an input yaw angle in the 0.orig/U file that changes the direction of the inlet flow. This will change the angle of flow: when the angle increases, it will decrease the flow in the +x direction and increase the flow in the +z direction using trigonometric relationships to give the desired inlet angle while maintaining the same velocity magnitude
This code also gets rid of the right and left side patches, and instead makes them into other faces of the inlet and the outlet. This is so that the flow can enter from two sides and leave from two sides.
Because this is how the yaw angle was achieved, the domain sizing in the z direction had to be increased as well, because now left of the building was acting in part as the upwind direction and right of the building as the downwind direction. The size and number of cells in those directions were adjusted in blockMesh.
Additionally, the refinement boxes in snappyHexMesh were modified to give the proper refinement regions for a max yaw case of 90 degrees providing sufficient upstream and cavitation buffer. This obviously ups the cell count a bit as it about quadruples the size of the refined regions, but it is not too extreme.
The yaw angle is changed within the O.orig/U file in the inlet condition, and the simulation can be run at yaw angles from 0 to 90 degrees.
IMPORTANTLY, especially if z components of the force are desired, I HIGHLY RECOMMEND that the shell spoiler geometries are used for all yaw cases, as the flow in the z direction will be significantly impacted by whether or not the spoiler is capped. A potentially interesting study could be to see the effect of a capped vs shell spoiler on the speedup for specifically yaw cases. The Shell spoiler geometry is located in the AccelerateWind OnShape in Ike > Spoilers


Potential Improvements:
 - The huge increase in needed refinement regions because of the yaw angle cases really drives up the cell count. These might be able to be reduced or parameterized within snappyHexMesh in order to make them appropriately sized for the case being ran.
For example, if a yaw case was being tested at only 10 deg, the full cavity refinement wouldn't be needed to the right of the building and this could be reduced. If there was a way to input the yaw angle and have that automatically resize the dimensions of the refinement boxes, that would be incredibly snazzy
 - In this thought, the blockMesh domains might also be changed in this same way, with the blockMesh only sizing the domain as needed
 - Although I'm not sure how it could be done, an easier way to avoid this large refinement would be to rotate the building and spoiler themselves in the mesh and change nothing else, keeping the inlet as blowing straight in. This would be an entirely different way of approaching the problem but would work. Make sure to update where the probes are as you do this, or rotate them with the building and spoiler.
